  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 20,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.069,
            0.156,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "112"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.477,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari |Grade 9| Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If D is outside the circle, then AD intersects the circle at E; see Fig. 5.27A. If D is inside the circle, extend AD to meet the circle at E; see Fig. 5.27B. Now C, E are on the same segment of the arc formed by chord AB. "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle \\mathrm{ACB}=\\angle \\mathrm{AEB}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.191,
            0.887,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If D were outside the circle, then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  AEB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an exterior angle of the ∆BED, so "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  AEB  > \\angle  ADB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Also, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (angles in the same segment of a circle), and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (given). This leads to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { A C B }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " being greater than itself, which is obviously not possible."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.273,
            0.886,
            0.332
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If D were inside as in Fig. 5.27B, then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  ADB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an exterior angle of BED. In the same way that we argued above, we reach an impossible conclusion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.337,
            0.886,
            0.397
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, we must eliminate both of these options (D being outside the circle and D being inside the circle). So, D must be on the circle passing through A, B, C. In other words, A, B, C, D are concyclic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.401,
            0.885,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When the vertices of a quadrilateral (which we also call a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4-gon",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ") are concyclic, the quadrilateral is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cyclic",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " quadrilateral."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.445,
            0.725,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We use this to show another nice property of circles!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.47,
            0.886,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "is 180°."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.525,
            0.489,
            0.743
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.169,
                0.525,
                0.489,
                0.743
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_20_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.272,
                0.75,
                0.354,
                0.768
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.28"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.512,
            0.586,
            0.877,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Given:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A, B, C, D are the vertices of a cyclic 4-gon (Fig. 5.28). This means that there is a circle passing through A, B, C, D. Let its centre be O."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.513,
            0.688,
            0.852,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To show:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BAD + \\angle BCD = 180^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.787,
            0.886,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How shall we show that this is true?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Consider arc BCD; point A is on the circle and it lies outside the arc BCD. So ∠BAD is half the angle that arc BCD subtends at the centre O. Since we move from OB to OD along C, this is the reflex angle BOD (dotted). So, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  BAD  = \\frac{1}{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (reflex angle BOD)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
